WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.380
If you remember taking calculus in high school, you probably remember, well, finding the slopes of curves and agonizing over limits and calculating rates of change.

00:10.700 --> 00:14.180
Oh, yeah. The classic trauma of high school math.

00:14.360 --> 00:20.960
Right. And you also probably thought you'd literally never use it again unless you became a physicist or an engineer.

00:21.800 --> 00:29.520
I mean, calculus feels very continuous, you know, very analog, heavily grounded in the physical laws of motion and all that.

00:29.520 --> 00:31.680
Exactly. It's the math of moving things.

00:32.040 --> 00:43.700
Yeah. But what if I told you that the exact same equations you use to pass high school math are secretly acting as the fundamental architectural blueprints for the software running on your computer right now?

00:43.960 --> 00:45.940
It is wild to think about.

00:46.140 --> 00:53.740
It is. The discrete, binary, completely rigid world of ones and zeros is actually governed by the chain rule and the product rule.

00:53.740 --> 01:03.060
So welcome to this deep dive. Today, we are unpacking a concept that bridges two disciplines that usually have absolutely nothing to do with each other.

01:03.340 --> 01:04.860
And we're going into the deep end today.

01:05.040 --> 01:11.560
We really are. We are going to look at what it literally means to take the mathematical derivative of a piece of software.

01:11.840 --> 01:17.320
Which is just a profound shift in perspective for anyone who codes or honestly, anyone who just uses technology.

01:17.320 --> 01:24.320
To guide you through this, we're drawing on this highly influential 2026 paper by the independent researcher Flyxion.

01:25.080 --> 01:27.500
It's titled Differential Calculus for Data Types.

01:27.640 --> 01:30.320
And it is dense, but we are going to break it all down for you.

01:30.500 --> 01:38.580
We are because it takes this incredibly theoretical mathematical crossover and applies it to the fundamental day-to-day problems of how software manages memory.

01:38.580 --> 01:44.260
But I should probably set a strict boundary right here at the start for what we are actually doing.

01:44.460 --> 01:45.500
Okay, good call. What's the ground rule?

01:45.760 --> 01:51.900
When we say calculus today, you have to throw out all the concepts of time, velocity, and acceleration.

01:52.180 --> 01:53.220
Okay, so no physics.

01:53.380 --> 02:00.000
Right, no physics. We aren't calculating the rate of change of a network's bandwidth or like how fast a processor is spinning. This is pure algebra.

02:00.300 --> 02:01.200
Just the mechanics of it.

02:01.420 --> 02:07.840
Exactly. We are taking the mechanical rules of differentiation and applying them to the physical shape and topology of computer data.

02:07.840 --> 02:09.600
Taking the derivative of software.

02:10.020 --> 02:15.860
I mean, it sounds like something straight out of a cyberpunk novel, but we are going to walk you through exactly how real it is.

02:16.220 --> 02:21.720
But to understand why any of this matters to you, we have to look at the foundational problem this solves in the first place.

02:22.040 --> 02:23.720
The why do we care part.

02:23.980 --> 02:30.040
Right. We have to look at what happens inside a computer's memory when you try to isolate a single piece of data.

02:30.520 --> 02:33.540
So let's start with a really good visual from the paper.

02:33.540 --> 02:37.140
However, imagine you have a sequence of data sitting in a computer's RAM.

02:37.580 --> 02:41.840
Just a very simple list of three elements. Let's call them A, B, and C.

02:42.060 --> 02:43.100
Okay. Simple enough.

02:43.600 --> 02:51.240
Your goal as the software program is to select the middle element, B, remove it, and do something with it.

02:51.440 --> 02:55.820
Maybe you are editing a document or updating a user profile, whatever.

02:55.820 --> 03:04.500
However, the critical challenge here is that you must retain exactly enough information to put B back into the exact same spot later.

03:05.100 --> 03:07.720
Or to replace it with a new element, like D, right?

03:08.040 --> 03:11.020
Exactly. Without destroying the rest of the list in the process.

03:11.480 --> 03:13.840
So you are basically poking a hole in the data.

03:13.840 --> 03:20.180
And to visualize the stakes here, you have to remember that memory in a computer isn't just floating around in a void.

03:20.600 --> 03:22.460
It's highly, highly structured.

03:22.960 --> 03:23.260
Very rigid.

03:23.740 --> 03:28.940
Yeah. Think of a massive, tightly packed, perfectly alphabetized library bookshelf.

03:29.660 --> 03:33.540
You have this long row of books wedged between two heavy bookends.

03:34.320 --> 03:40.100
If you need to pull a specific book out from the middle of the shelf to read it, you create an empty space. A hole.

03:40.400 --> 03:42.340
Right. And the environment doesn't like holes.

03:42.340 --> 03:48.240
No, it doesn't. If you just walk away with the book, the structural integrity of that shelf is totally compromised.

03:49.020 --> 03:54.180
The books on either side might fall over, or someone might come along and push the remaining books together to close the gap.

03:55.160 --> 04:00.080
The environment doesn't naturally remember where that specific book came from once it's gone.

04:00.720 --> 04:03.720
The structure entirely loses its original state.

04:04.300 --> 04:07.700
And you know, in the early days of programming, this was a massive source of bugs.

04:07.840 --> 04:08.240
Oh, I bet.

04:08.240 --> 04:16.100
Yeah. If a program lost track of where a piece of data belonged, you'd get memory leaks, segmentation faults, totally corrupted databases.

04:16.660 --> 04:19.280
Pointers would just point to dead, empty space.

04:19.360 --> 04:21.800
Right. The dreaded no-pointer exception.

04:21.800 --> 04:34.220
So, to ensure you can put that exact book back, or to put a revised edition of that book back into the correct alphabetical slot, you can't just memorize the title of the book you took.

04:34.660 --> 04:35.540
That's not enough.

04:35.740 --> 04:36.360
Not even close.

04:36.700 --> 04:42.740
You have to retain the exact stack of books to the left of the hole, and the exact stack of books to the right of the hole.

04:42.740 --> 04:48.560
And in Flyxion's paper, this leftover environment is formally defined as a context.

04:48.780 --> 04:49.360
A context.

04:49.700 --> 04:49.860
Yeah.

04:50.180 --> 04:56.840
When you remove B from your list of A, B, C, you are left with two distinct pieces of structural data in memory.

04:57.240 --> 05:04.560
You have a prefix, which is the sequence containing just A, and you have a suffix, which is the sequence containing just C.

05:04.780 --> 05:07.440
And those two things together make up the context.

05:07.440 --> 05:15.180
Exactly. That pair, the prefix, and the suffix together is the exact minimum information required for what the paper calls reconstruction.

05:15.760 --> 05:18.880
You need both sides to rebuild the original list around the hole.

05:19.260 --> 05:22.960
Well, that feels pretty intuitive for a flat, simple list of three things.

05:23.100 --> 05:28.360
You just look at it, grab the middle, and the left side is the prefix, the right side is the suffix. Done.

05:29.120 --> 05:29.820
Simple enough.

05:30.180 --> 05:32.940
But software today isn't built on flat lists.

05:32.940 --> 05:37.580
It relies on massive, nested, incredibly complex data structures.

05:37.900 --> 05:43.440
We are talking about trees with millions of branches, recursive loops, multidimensional arrays.

05:43.780 --> 05:44.960
It gets messy fast.

05:45.280 --> 05:50.540
So how do you systematically define the exact shape of a hole when the data is that complex?

05:51.280 --> 05:55.420
Like, can you mathematically calculate a context for absolutely any data type?

05:55.420 --> 05:59.840
That right there is the driving question of the research, and the answer is yes.

06:00.460 --> 06:08.700
But to perform that calculation, to actually derive the context systematically, we first have to translate computer science into mathematical algebra.

06:09.040 --> 06:10.360
We have to build a bridge.

06:10.720 --> 06:12.880
We have to build a new dictionary, really.

06:12.880 --> 06:17.960
I want to walk through this dictionary carefully, because we are essentially learning a new language here.

06:18.580 --> 06:20.040
And I don't want to lose you if you're listening.

06:21.060 --> 06:23.540
Walk us through the basic grammar the paper provides.

06:23.740 --> 06:27.280
We need to map programming concepts directly to math symbols.

06:27.620 --> 06:30.960
Okay, so the paper outlines a very elegant type algebra.

06:31.480 --> 06:33.540
We start with the absolute base states.

06:33.920 --> 06:35.500
First, we have the number zero.

06:36.300 --> 06:37.640
Just a standard zero.

06:37.940 --> 06:38.200
Right.

06:38.640 --> 06:41.620
In this algebra, zero represents a type with no inhabitants.

06:41.620 --> 06:45.840
It's a logical impossibility, a state that cannot exist or hold data.

06:46.420 --> 06:49.660
Wait, what's the practical use of an impossible state?

06:50.080 --> 06:50.940
Why even define it?

06:51.420 --> 06:57.420
Well, in programming, it represents a function that never returns, or like an error state that crashes the program entirely.

06:57.920 --> 06:59.120
It's a structural dead end.

06:59.340 --> 07:00.500
You can't get data from it.

07:00.660 --> 07:01.480
Oh, okay.

07:02.100 --> 07:02.660
That makes sense.

07:02.880 --> 07:04.420
Next, we have the number one.

07:04.680 --> 07:05.320
The standard one?

07:05.540 --> 07:06.980
This represents a unit type.

07:06.980 --> 07:13.600
It has a single inhabitant, which means it carries a static state, but it contributes no positions for you to select.

07:13.600 --> 07:15.940
So, zero is empty and impossible.

07:16.360 --> 07:16.680
Mm-hmm.

07:16.800 --> 07:23.680
One is a solid block, like a termination point or a null pointer at the very end of a list.

07:23.960 --> 07:24.400
Exactly.

07:25.020 --> 07:31.460
It takes up space in the logic, but if I'm looking to pluck a specific piece of data out, I can't grab anything from a one.

07:31.800 --> 07:32.240
Precisely.

07:32.500 --> 07:36.220
It is structurally present, but completely invisible to the selection process.

07:36.220 --> 07:40.380
Now we move to how we combine things, which is where the algebra gets really interesting.

07:40.780 --> 07:42.020
We use the plus sign.

07:42.500 --> 07:42.800
Addition.

07:43.280 --> 07:48.740
Yes, but in our data type algebra, the plus sign represents a strict disjoint choice between two types.

07:48.980 --> 07:54.020
If we write F plus G, it means your data structure is either an F or a G.

07:54.380 --> 07:55.440
Wait, I have to stop you there.

07:56.340 --> 07:59.620
Adding types makes no logical sense to me at first glance.

07:59.860 --> 08:00.840
I know, it feels weird.

08:01.080 --> 08:01.540
It does.

08:01.840 --> 08:05.560
Because in standard math, if I add two and three, I get five.

08:06.220 --> 08:09.000
I get a completely merged new numerical value.

08:09.500 --> 08:11.840
The inputs completely lose their individual identity.

08:12.460 --> 08:17.420
How does a plus sign suddenly translate to a strict OR choice in programming?

08:17.780 --> 08:20.080
Those feel like totally contradictory concepts.

08:20.460 --> 08:23.480
It is a very fair point of friction, and a lot of people get stuck there.

08:24.080 --> 08:29.020
You have to think of it not as arithmetic addition, but as an algebraic sum type.

08:29.480 --> 08:30.860
Think of it in terms of set theory.

08:30.860 --> 08:31.240
Okay.

08:31.660 --> 08:32.160
Set theory.

08:32.700 --> 08:34.680
If you have a set of apples, we'll call that F,

08:34.680 --> 08:46.900
F, and a disjoint set of oranges, G, the sum of those sets, F plus G, represents a basket where any single item you pull out is strictly guaranteed to be either an apple or an orange.

08:47.100 --> 08:48.120
It's a tagged union.

08:48.460 --> 08:49.700
Ah, okay.

08:50.380 --> 08:51.340
A tagged union.

08:51.340 --> 08:53.940
Let me make that concrete for you listening.

08:54.580 --> 08:58.600
Imagine you are writing software for a vehicle registration system.

08:59.280 --> 09:11.400
A vehicle entry in your database can be either a car, which has specific data fields for number of doors and trunk space, or a motorcycle, which has completely different specific fields for handlebar type.

09:12.120 --> 09:12.360
Right.

09:12.680 --> 09:13.380
They don't mix.

09:13.380 --> 09:21.440
The plus sign is the mathematical way of saying this block of memory holds one or the other, but absolutely never both simultaneously.

09:21.900 --> 09:23.140
That is a perfect translation.

09:23.600 --> 09:26.780
The plus sign is a fork in the structural road.

09:26.780 --> 09:32.620
Now, the counterpart to that is the asterisk, representing a product or multiplication.

09:33.440 --> 09:34.740
So if plus means or?

09:35.320 --> 09:36.900
Multiplication means and.

09:37.460 --> 09:39.940
F times G represents a pair.

09:40.100 --> 09:44.600
You have an F and you have a G, sitting adjacent to each other in memory.

09:44.960 --> 09:50.160
So going back to the vehicle database, a specific car has an engine and a chassis.

09:50.680 --> 09:54.000
Those two distinct pieces of data exist together at the same time.

09:54.000 --> 09:57.520
In programming, that's a struct, a record, or a tuple.

09:58.540 --> 09:58.820
Exactly.

09:59.540 --> 10:06.660
Okay, so we have zero for impossible, one for a solid unit, plus for or, and multiply for and.

10:06.980 --> 10:09.500
But what is the actual data we are trying to manipulate?

10:09.720 --> 10:11.820
What is the literal book we want to pull off the shelf?

10:12.500 --> 10:16.140
The target data is represented by the capital letter X.

10:16.680 --> 10:18.540
X is the variable in our algebra.

10:18.540 --> 10:23.000
It represents the actual occurrences of data that are eligible for selection.

10:23.000 --> 10:28.800
When we talk about poking a hole in a data structure, we are looking for an X to remove.

10:29.700 --> 10:30.960
Okay, X is the target.

10:31.060 --> 10:35.040
Let's put this dictionary to work, because I want to see this calculus in action.

10:35.600 --> 10:41.920
If X is a single slot for a piece of data, what does a formula like X squared actually mean?

10:42.960 --> 10:46.200
Because obviously you can't multiply a user ID by itself.

10:46.200 --> 10:48.880
Let's break down X squared using our new dictionary.

10:49.440 --> 10:53.460
Algebraically, X squared is just X multiplied by X, X times X.

10:53.920 --> 10:56.480
We defined multiplication as a product, an and.

10:56.700 --> 11:04.560
Therefore, X squared simply represents a data structure consisting of two identical data slots sitting side by side, a pair of X's.

11:04.560 --> 11:10.960
Got it. If X is a single floating point number, X squared is a coordinate pair, like a longitude and latitude.

11:11.420 --> 11:17.380
It is not a mathematical squaring in the value itself. It is the structural shape of two things paired together.

11:17.780 --> 11:19.800
Yes, it's the topology of the data.

11:20.320 --> 11:23.160
Now, here is where Flyxion bridges the gap to calculus.

11:23.300 --> 11:23.640
Here we go.

11:23.640 --> 11:26.720
We have our data type, X squared, a pair of slots.

11:27.480 --> 11:31.080
Let's reach into the traditional calculus toolkit that everyone learns in school.

11:31.500 --> 11:38.000
If I ask you to take the formal derivative of the function X squared with respect to X, what is the result?

11:38.260 --> 11:42.860
Okay, the power rule. You bring the exponent down to the front, subtract 1 from the exponent.

11:43.380 --> 11:45.700
The derivative of X squared is 2X.

11:45.940 --> 11:48.420
The derivative of X squared is 2X.

11:49.060 --> 11:52.660
Now write 2X out algebraically without using the multiplier.

11:52.660 --> 11:55.700
Just addition. It's X plus X.

11:56.000 --> 11:58.520
The derivative of X squared is X plus X.

11:58.860 --> 12:03.720
Now, take that mathematical result and translate it back into our programming dictionary.

12:04.240 --> 12:08.900
What does X plus X actually mean in the physical context of our computer memory?

12:09.620 --> 12:11.440
Okay, let's trace this carefully.

12:12.720 --> 12:14.020
X is a data slot.

12:14.860 --> 12:16.560
The plus sign means or.

12:17.400 --> 12:20.420
So, X plus X means you have a choice.

12:20.420 --> 12:25.360
Either an intact X on the left or an intact X on the right.

12:25.360 --> 12:27.960
Let's connect this back to our primary mission.

12:28.400 --> 12:35.300
Our goal was to systematically derive the context, the exact shape of the hole left behind when we select one X out of our structure.

12:35.900 --> 12:38.220
Our starting structure was X squared, a pair.

12:38.220 --> 12:41.640
I see where this is going, and it is honestly startling.

12:42.460 --> 12:44.640
Let me walk through the physical mechanics of this.

12:45.460 --> 12:48.500
We started with a pair of slots, X and X.

12:49.080 --> 12:50.400
I want to pull one of them out.

12:51.100 --> 12:54.960
If I poke a hole and pull out the left X, what am I left with in memory?

12:55.480 --> 12:56.620
You're left with the right one.

12:56.620 --> 12:57.200
Right.

12:57.600 --> 13:00.280
I'm left with the intact X on the right.

13:01.180 --> 13:06.160
Conversely, if I pull out the right X, I am left with the intact X on the left.

13:06.700 --> 13:13.440
So, my leftover environment, my context, is either the right X or the left X.

13:13.760 --> 13:15.000
X plus X.

13:15.000 --> 13:25.840
Taking the traditional mathematical derivative of the pair X squared, literally output the exact two possible structural contexts we can have, which is X plus X.

13:26.560 --> 13:31.360
The calculus just gave us the precise blueprint for how the data must behave when edited.

13:31.840 --> 13:37.540
This is the core isomorphism of the paper, and it's vital to grasp that it is not an analogy.

13:37.720 --> 13:38.980
It is not a cute metaphor.

13:38.980 --> 13:48.220
The mathematical derivative of the data type's structural formula yields the exact literal structural formula of its one whole context.

13:48.840 --> 13:50.740
That works flawlessly for a simple pair.

13:51.100 --> 13:53.380
But X squared is like day one of a calculus class.

13:53.620 --> 13:54.340
How does this scale?

13:54.760 --> 13:58.220
If this is a universal truth, it has to apply to the foundational rules of calculus.

13:58.740 --> 14:01.060
How does it handle the sum rule or the product rule?

14:01.540 --> 14:02.500
It maps perfectly.

14:02.880 --> 14:04.200
Let's look at the sum rule first.

14:04.200 --> 14:10.240
In calculus, the derivative of F plus G is the derivative of F plus the derivative of G.

14:10.600 --> 14:14.860
Or mathematically, F plus G prime equals F prime plus G prime.

14:15.320 --> 14:19.780
Translating that, F plus G means our data is a choice, a car or a motorcycle.

14:20.520 --> 14:25.020
So, if we want to poke a hole in that vehicle data, the rule says the context is F prime plus G prime.

14:25.380 --> 14:30.060
The context is either a hole inside a car structure or a hole inside a motorcycle structure.

14:30.540 --> 14:31.720
That tracks logically.

14:31.720 --> 14:34.940
If the original data was a car, the hole obviously has to be in the car.

14:35.560 --> 14:37.200
The sum rule is very straightforward.

14:37.620 --> 14:42.960
The product rule is where the descriptive power of this mathematical mapping truly shows its utility for software engineering.

14:43.420 --> 14:45.400
Do you recall the formula for the product rule?

14:45.860 --> 14:46.560
I do, yeah.

14:46.980 --> 14:57.220
The derivative of F times G is the derivative of F times G plus F times the derivative of G.

14:57.820 --> 15:00.720
So, F prime times G plus F times G prime.

15:00.720 --> 15:13.320
Now, let's interpret that formula not as a math problem on a chalkboard, but as a set of strict logical instructions for a computer navigating a pair of massive data structures in RAM, F and G.

15:13.660 --> 15:13.900
Okay.

15:14.440 --> 15:16.760
Let me break down the physical memory mechanics of this.

15:17.720 --> 15:22.120
Imagine F and G are two very large blocks of data sitting next to each other.

15:22.120 --> 15:27.480
Maybe F is a massive text document, and G is a high-res image attached to it.

15:28.100 --> 15:28.880
They are paired.

15:29.380 --> 15:30.120
F times G.

15:30.340 --> 15:30.580
Right.

15:30.720 --> 15:31.460
They exist together.

15:31.840 --> 15:35.000
I need to dive in and edit one specific byte of data.

15:35.460 --> 15:36.580
I'm making one whole.

15:37.440 --> 15:39.100
How does the computer handle the context?

15:39.800 --> 15:40.720
Well, look at the formula.

15:41.120 --> 15:43.500
F prime times G plus F times G prime.

15:44.160 --> 15:47.320
The plus sign in the middle is the or choice.

15:47.700 --> 15:48.140
Exactly.

15:49.180 --> 15:50.240
Choice one, the left side.

15:51.000 --> 15:53.620
I edit a byte inside the text document, F.

15:54.400 --> 15:55.920
What does my memory need to preserve?

15:56.260 --> 15:58.580
It needs to hold the text document with the hole in it.

15:58.800 --> 15:59.640
That's F prime.

16:00.400 --> 16:04.100
And it has to keep the entire attached image completely intact in memory.

16:04.620 --> 16:05.420
That's the G.

16:05.980 --> 16:07.820
So, we get F prime times G.

16:09.260 --> 16:10.180
And choice two?

16:10.740 --> 16:11.840
Choice two, the right side.

16:11.840 --> 16:14.200
I edit a pixel in the image.

16:14.660 --> 16:15.040
G.

16:15.940 --> 16:17.000
What does the memory do?

16:17.480 --> 16:21.060
It preserves the entire text document perfectly intact, which is F.

16:21.520 --> 16:24.960
And it holds the image with a missing pixel, which is G prime.

16:25.260 --> 16:26.440
So, we get F times G prime.

16:27.540 --> 16:32.760
The calculus formula perfectly describes every single valid state the computer's memory can be in

16:32.760 --> 16:35.020
after one piece of data is selected.

16:35.360 --> 16:37.560
And the paper emphasizes a major claim here.

16:37.560 --> 16:43.220
This familiar differentiation rule is actively specifying what a program must do during reconstruction.

16:43.780 --> 16:44.660
It's writing the color.

16:45.220 --> 16:46.060
Basically, yeah.

16:46.480 --> 16:48.880
The plus sign in the middle of the formula acts as a tag.

16:49.360 --> 16:52.700
It tells the software which side, the left structure, or the right structure, is supposed

16:52.700 --> 16:54.180
to receive the replacement data.

16:54.720 --> 16:58.320
Whichever factor doesn't have the prime, the intact factor, just supplies the rest of

16:58.320 --> 16:59.380
the unedited structure.

17:00.000 --> 17:03.740
The derivative literally writes the if-else logic for the software.

17:03.740 --> 17:06.820
The calculus rule isn't just describing the shape.

17:07.180 --> 17:11.820
It is giving you the exact conditional logic required to manage the memory safely.

17:12.200 --> 17:13.900
Which is incredibly powerful.

17:14.300 --> 17:15.480
But let's push this further.

17:16.160 --> 17:20.160
So far, we've only dealt with finite fixed structures, pairs, and choices.

17:20.720 --> 17:24.840
But the real power of modern software comes from recursive data.

17:25.200 --> 17:26.180
Ah, recursion.

17:26.480 --> 17:31.620
Data that loops structures that can grow dynamically in memory, like a list where you can keep clicking

17:31.620 --> 17:33.380
add item indefinitely.

17:33.860 --> 17:37.600
If a structure doesn't have a fixed size, how can you possibly take its derivative?

17:38.140 --> 17:40.240
This is the critical threshold of Flyxion's research.

17:40.440 --> 17:43.520
We are moving into recursive types, specifically the list.

17:43.920 --> 17:47.480
We have to define a list mathematically before we can differentiate it.

17:47.700 --> 17:52.040
So how do we describe a finite list of elements, where each element is of type x?

17:52.260 --> 17:53.200
How do we write that out?

17:53.200 --> 17:54.840
We use a recursive equation.

17:55.220 --> 17:57.880
L of x equals 1 plus x times L of x.

17:57.880 --> 18:02.080
Let's translate this equation together for the listener, because understanding this is

18:02.080 --> 18:03.320
crucial to the whole concept.

18:04.060 --> 18:05.500
L of x is our list.

18:06.300 --> 18:08.500
The equal sign means is defined as.

18:08.980 --> 18:12.080
So, a list is defined as 1.

18:12.640 --> 18:16.640
And we remember 1 means a solid unit, an empty termination state.

18:16.880 --> 18:17.880
Right, the end of the line.

18:18.040 --> 18:19.940
Then we have the plus sign, which means or.

18:19.940 --> 18:23.240
Then we have x times L of x.

18:23.660 --> 18:24.980
X is our target data slot.

18:25.320 --> 18:27.880
The asterisk is and, a pair.

18:28.640 --> 18:30.840
And L of x is another list.

18:31.060 --> 18:31.300
Yes.

18:32.220 --> 18:33.320
Reading it in plain English.

18:34.200 --> 18:39.860
A list is either an empty termination point, or it is a single piece of data paired with

18:39.860 --> 18:40.680
another list.

18:41.080 --> 18:45.560
This is stunning, because that is exactly how a computer builds a linked list in RAM.

18:46.080 --> 18:47.980
You don't just dump all the items in a bucket.

18:48.220 --> 18:49.000
No, you chain them.

18:49.000 --> 18:54.040
You create a node with a value, x, and that node holds a memory pointer directed at the

18:54.040 --> 18:54.580
next node.

18:55.120 --> 18:58.100
That next node is the start of the rest of the list.

18:58.700 --> 19:02.980
And at the very end, the last pointer just points to a null space, which is our 1.

19:03.280 --> 19:08.140
You've perfectly captured the recursive physical reality of a linked list.

19:08.940 --> 19:11.200
Now, we perform the mathematical operation.

19:11.460 --> 19:15.660
We differentiate this entire recursive equation with respect to x.

19:15.660 --> 19:18.260
We just apply the rules we already established.

19:18.420 --> 19:18.620
Okay.

19:18.820 --> 19:19.660
Let's walk through it.

19:20.280 --> 19:23.040
The derivative of L of x becomes L prime of x.

19:23.320 --> 19:28.540
The derivative of 1 is 0, because an empty termination state has no x to select.

19:29.000 --> 19:30.180
Then we hit the plus sign.

19:30.740 --> 19:32.880
Then we have x times L of x.

19:33.380 --> 19:34.720
This is a product, an and.

19:34.720 --> 19:40.640
So we must use the product rule we just talked about, f prime times g plus f times g prime.

19:40.640 --> 19:44.800
Okay, let's work through the product rule on x times L of x.

19:45.320 --> 19:48.720
Our f is the single piece of data, x.

19:49.260 --> 19:52.420
Our g is the rest of the list, L of x.

19:52.960 --> 19:54.400
First part of the product rule.

19:55.060 --> 19:57.880
The derivative of f times g intact.

19:58.620 --> 20:01.580
The derivative of x is just 1, a single whole.

20:02.140 --> 20:04.360
1 times L of x is just L of x.

20:04.680 --> 20:07.620
So the first part of our result is L of x.

20:07.980 --> 20:08.180
Good.

20:08.420 --> 20:09.340
Now the second half.

20:09.340 --> 20:14.060
Second part of the product rule, f intact times the derivative of g.

20:14.660 --> 20:19.480
That means x is left alone, and we multiply it by the derivative of the rest of the list,

20:19.560 --> 20:21.180
which is L prime of x.

20:21.440 --> 20:23.840
So we get x times L prime of x.

20:24.400 --> 20:30.100
Putting the whole equation back together, the derivative of our list is L prime of x equals

20:30.100 --> 20:34.440
0 plus L of x plus x times L prime of x.

20:34.740 --> 20:36.100
And we can drop the 0.

20:36.440 --> 20:36.740
Right.

20:36.740 --> 20:42.800
Drop the 0, leaving us with L prime of x equals L of x plus x times L prime of x.

20:42.800 --> 20:47.640
Now let's translate that final mathematical derivative back into operational English.

20:47.640 --> 20:50.900
What is this equation actively telling a computer to do?

20:51.180 --> 20:53.020
Let's read it left to right as an algorithm.

20:53.860 --> 20:57.820
L prime of x means I am currently searching for a hole in a list.

20:58.480 --> 21:00.900
The equal sign means here are my options.

21:01.520 --> 21:03.960
First we have L of x on the left side of the plus.

21:04.180 --> 21:05.080
What does that signify?

21:05.460 --> 21:06.920
It means I found the hole immediately.

21:06.920 --> 21:11.380
I poked a hole in the very first element, the head of the list, and what remains.

21:12.280 --> 21:16.260
I'm left with L of x, the entire rest of the list perfectly intact.

21:16.680 --> 21:17.320
The tail.

21:17.580 --> 21:18.020
Exactly.

21:18.420 --> 21:20.900
But we have the plus sign, the or.

21:21.320 --> 21:24.440
So, or, I didn't find the hole in the head.

21:24.980 --> 21:26.420
I look at the right side of the plus.

21:27.200 --> 21:28.940
x times L prime of x.

21:29.720 --> 21:33.140
This means I keep the current head of the list completely intact in memory.

21:33.500 --> 21:34.300
That's the x.

21:34.980 --> 21:37.900
And I continue my search for the hole in the rest of the list.

21:38.100 --> 21:39.560
That's the L prime of x.

21:40.180 --> 21:42.000
This isn't just a physical description.

21:42.660 --> 21:47.620
This equation is literally the step-by-step software algorithm for traversing a linked list.

21:48.140 --> 21:48.640
Check the head.

21:48.920 --> 21:51.300
If it's not there, save the head and check the tail.

21:51.540 --> 21:55.720
It is the algorithmic traversal mapped perfectly to algebra.

21:55.720 --> 21:59.800
If the selected position is the head, the retained data is the tail.

22:00.120 --> 22:05.660
If the selected position is further down, you preserve the head intact and recurse into the tail.

22:06.120 --> 22:08.720
But consider the reality of the data structure.

22:09.240 --> 22:13.800
Because this list is finite, this recursive search cannot continue infinitely.

22:14.160 --> 22:15.320
It must resolve at some point.

22:15.560 --> 22:15.660
Right.

22:16.680 --> 22:20.700
Eventually, the computer finds the specific x it wants to select.

22:21.220 --> 22:25.060
It makes the hole, and the recursive search just terminates.

22:25.060 --> 22:30.140
And when it terminates, look at what has accumulated in the computer's memory during the search.

22:30.940 --> 22:37.720
Every time the algorithm moves past an element without selecting it, it preserves an intact x on the left side of the operation.

22:38.380 --> 22:41.720
It is effectively building a pile of bypassed elements behind it.

22:41.840 --> 22:43.700
Like a trail of breadcrumbs.

22:44.080 --> 22:44.560
Exactly.

22:45.340 --> 22:49.840
These retained heads, read in chronological order, form a new list of their own.

22:50.120 --> 22:50.840
They form the prefix.

22:50.840 --> 22:54.880
And when you hit your target and stop, what remains in front of you?

22:55.420 --> 22:57.860
The remaining intact portion of the original list.

22:58.540 --> 22:59.360
The suffix.

22:59.360 --> 23:10.700
So, after the mathematical recursion fully plays out, the memory holds a list of the elements you passed, the prefix, and the list of elements you haven't touched yet, the suffix.

23:11.320 --> 23:19.060
Therefore, mathematically, the derivative of a list, L prime of x, resolves perfectly to L of x times L of x.

23:19.060 --> 23:19.980
Two lists.

23:20.520 --> 23:22.600
A prefix list and a suffix list.

23:23.200 --> 23:24.620
Or simply L squared.

23:25.420 --> 23:26.340
L squared.

23:26.920 --> 23:31.740
The context of a whole in a one-dimensional list mathematically reduces to a pair of lists.

23:32.200 --> 23:35.220
The stuff before the whole and the stuff after the whole.

23:35.760 --> 23:36.380
It's beautiful.

23:37.020 --> 23:37.540
It is.

23:37.540 --> 23:44.900
I want to pause and really reflect on why Flyxion dedicates so much space in the paper to proving this specifically.

23:45.740 --> 23:54.780
Because when we started this discussion, we talked about the prefix and suffix saving the books to the left and right of the whole as if it were just a clever engineering solution.

23:55.100 --> 23:55.180
Right.

23:55.280 --> 23:55.880
Like a hack.

23:56.260 --> 23:56.440
Yeah.

23:56.800 --> 24:03.580
It felt like an early programmer looked at a memory leak and thought, oh, if I just save the left side and the right side, the list won't collapse.

24:04.140 --> 24:05.120
It felt subjective.

24:05.320 --> 24:06.780
An invention of pure convenience.

24:06.780 --> 24:11.060
But this derivation proves that the programmer didn't invent anything.

24:11.380 --> 24:11.740
Not at all.

24:12.580 --> 24:17.840
The prefix-suffix context is mathematically mandated by the topological definition of the list.

24:18.720 --> 24:23.800
The math proves that L squared is the fundamental unavoidable truth of the data structure.

24:24.380 --> 24:28.220
The programmer was just discovering the physical constraints dictated by the algebra.

24:28.860 --> 24:31.340
That is the philosophical core of Flyxion's work.

24:31.540 --> 24:35.000
Software engineering isn't just inventing arbitrary logic to get things to work.

24:35.000 --> 24:37.400
It's discovering the geometric laws of data.

24:38.120 --> 24:39.280
That's a great way to put it.

24:39.280 --> 24:42.600
Now, a list is beautiful, but it is strictly one-dimensional.

24:42.980 --> 24:44.960
You just keep moving forward, node to node.

24:45.640 --> 24:47.740
But look at the file system on your computer right now.

24:47.880 --> 24:49.000
It's not a flat list.

24:49.160 --> 24:51.280
It's folders inside of folders inside of folders.

24:51.840 --> 24:53.500
Look at how a web page renders.

24:53.640 --> 24:56.660
It's a massive branching document object model.

24:56.660 --> 24:57.640
Everything is a tree.

24:57.640 --> 24:58.600
Everything.

24:59.400 --> 25:02.540
So what happens to our elegant algebra when the data branches?

25:03.280 --> 25:09.120
Does this simple plus-and-multiply system break down when we introduce a complex tree structure?

25:09.520 --> 25:11.860
Because navigating a straight list is simple.

25:12.180 --> 25:12.980
You just go forward.

25:13.760 --> 25:19.220
Navigating a massive branching tree, where every single point offers multiple diverging paths,

25:19.680 --> 25:21.280
sounds like an algorithmic nightmare.

25:21.780 --> 25:24.280
How does calculus handle navigating a maze?

25:24.280 --> 25:29.980
To navigate a maze, we need the heaviest tool in the calculus toolkit, the chain rule.

25:30.100 --> 25:30.620
The chain rule.

25:31.020 --> 25:35.960
The chain rule is designed specifically for nested functions, functions inside of functions.

25:36.500 --> 25:40.620
In traditional calculus, if you want the derivative of f of g of x,

25:40.800 --> 25:43.660
written as f parenthesis g parenthesis x,

25:44.540 --> 25:47.940
the rule dictates you take the derivative of the outer function,

25:48.260 --> 25:52.560
evaluated at the inner function, multiplied by the derivative of the inner function.

25:52.560 --> 25:58.380
So mathematically, f prime of g of x times g prime of x.

25:58.480 --> 25:58.900
Exactly.

25:59.720 --> 26:01.420
Let's ground this with an analogy,

26:01.980 --> 26:05.840
because nested functions can get really abstract really quickly.

26:06.500 --> 26:11.740
Think of zooming into a fractal, or like the movie Inception, dreams inside of dreams.

26:11.880 --> 26:12.580
Great analogy.

26:12.580 --> 26:17.040
Imagine you were trying to find a specific text file on your computer,

26:17.380 --> 26:22.460
but it's buried deep inside a project folder, which is compressed inside a zip file,

26:22.880 --> 26:24.480
which is inside your downloads folder.

26:25.080 --> 26:29.200
That is a nested data type, f of g of x.

26:29.880 --> 26:34.860
To find that specific text file, to poke our hole deep inside this nested structure,

26:35.420 --> 26:38.420
the computer can't just magically teleport to the data.

26:38.820 --> 26:40.720
It has to physically navigate the layers.

26:40.720 --> 26:42.400
Layer by layer.

26:43.020 --> 26:45.700
First, you have to navigate the outer structure.

26:46.240 --> 26:48.080
You have to find the write and zip file and open it.

26:48.520 --> 26:52.200
When you open it, your memory leaves behind an outer context,

26:52.460 --> 26:55.720
all the other files and folders in the downloads directory that you didn't open.

26:56.220 --> 26:58.860
That outer context is the f prime.

26:59.500 --> 26:59.720
Right.

27:00.320 --> 27:02.860
Then you dive into the inner structure, the g.

27:02.860 --> 27:08.060
You find the specific text file inside the zip, leaving behind an inner context,

27:08.560 --> 27:10.660
the other files inside the zip that you ignored.

27:11.340 --> 27:12.400
That's the g prime.

27:13.180 --> 27:17.300
Because they are nested, when you want to back out and reconstruct the file path,

27:17.480 --> 27:19.920
you must do it in exact reverse order.

27:20.700 --> 27:24.780
You have to seal up the inner folder first, then you seal up the outer folder.

27:24.780 --> 27:30.000
And the necessity of reverse order reconstruction is exactly what the chain rule captures

27:30.000 --> 27:31.700
with its multiplication of contexts.

27:32.500 --> 27:38.260
Now, let's apply this mathematical nesting to a highly specific, widely used branching structure,

27:38.660 --> 27:39.720
a binary tree.

27:40.040 --> 27:40.940
Okay, let's do it.

27:41.340 --> 27:44.800
We define our binary tree recursively, similarly to the list.

27:44.800 --> 27:52.260
First, a tree, t of x, is defined as t of x equals x plus t of x times t of x.

27:52.980 --> 27:57.780
Translating the algebra again, t of x is our tree, the pluses are or.

27:58.200 --> 28:02.700
So a binary tree is either x, which means it's just a single piece of data,

28:03.160 --> 28:04.620
a leaf at the very end of a branch.

28:04.900 --> 28:05.520
The dead end.

28:05.760 --> 28:05.960
Right.

28:06.360 --> 28:09.160
Or it's t of x times t of x.

28:09.500 --> 28:13.340
It's a pair of two other trees, a left branch and a right branch.

28:13.340 --> 28:17.100
That perfectly describes a binary tree in computer science.

28:17.360 --> 28:20.080
Every node is either a dead end leaf containing data,

28:20.600 --> 28:24.040
or a fork in the road, splitting into a left path and a right path.

28:24.520 --> 28:25.060
Exactly.

28:25.760 --> 28:28.320
When we differentiate this recursive tree equation,

28:28.900 --> 28:31.640
we must apply both the chain rule for the nesting

28:31.640 --> 28:36.560
and what Flyxion outlines as proposition one, the fixed point rule.

28:37.160 --> 28:40.140
The mathematical derivative yields something far more complex

28:40.140 --> 28:42.480
than the simple prefix suffix of a list.

28:42.480 --> 28:46.880
It yields a unit case, which represents finally arriving at the leaf

28:46.880 --> 28:50.620
you were searching for, along with a series of structural cases

28:50.620 --> 28:52.220
that act as breadcrumbs.

28:52.340 --> 28:52.780
Breadcrumbs.

28:53.000 --> 28:53.940
Think back to the maze.

28:54.540 --> 28:55.980
When you take a left turn at a fork,

28:56.440 --> 29:00.340
what information must you retain to be able to rebuild the maze behind you

29:00.340 --> 29:01.020
when you leave?

29:01.760 --> 29:04.420
Simply remembering, I went left, is insufficient.

29:04.860 --> 29:06.680
Let's dig into the memory mechanics of that,

29:07.300 --> 29:09.640
because this is where the theory actually hits the metal.

29:09.640 --> 29:13.640
A path in a data structure isn't just a list of textual directions

29:13.640 --> 29:16.060
on a piece of paper, like go left, then go right.

29:16.480 --> 29:17.680
No, it's physical memory.

29:17.900 --> 29:18.100
Right.

29:18.600 --> 29:22.520
If you just have textual directions and you pull the data out,

29:23.020 --> 29:25.780
the unreferenced branches of the tree just get swept away

29:25.780 --> 29:27.200
by the computer's garbage collector.

29:27.740 --> 29:29.320
The whole tree collapses into nothing.

29:29.320 --> 29:33.700
In a physical data context, the path you record must carry

29:33.700 --> 29:37.380
the actual memory addresses and the physical data

29:37.380 --> 29:39.340
of the branches you bypassed.

29:39.700 --> 29:40.020
Exactly.

29:40.380 --> 29:43.400
If I arrive at a fork and I go down the left branch,

29:43.880 --> 29:46.960
my context has to store the fact that I went left,

29:47.500 --> 29:50.420
and it has to literally hold onto the memory pointer

29:50.420 --> 29:52.560
for the entire right branch of the tree.

29:52.560 --> 29:56.580
I have to physically drag the unexplored sibling data

29:56.580 --> 29:57.880
with me down the hole.

29:58.420 --> 30:00.480
That way, when I'm done editing the leaf

30:00.480 --> 30:02.600
and I'm walking backward out of the maze,

30:02.780 --> 30:06.080
I can snap that massive bright branch back onto the node

30:06.080 --> 30:06.780
as I pass it.

30:07.320 --> 30:09.880
You are beautifully articulating the physical reality

30:09.880 --> 30:11.160
of the fixed point rule.

30:11.800 --> 30:14.320
For any recursive type, the mathematical derivative

30:14.320 --> 30:16.980
strictly factors into two distinct components.

30:17.180 --> 30:20.780
It yields a local factor paired with a stack of steps.

30:20.780 --> 30:22.800
A local factor and a stack.

30:23.100 --> 30:23.380
Yes.

30:23.860 --> 30:25.880
The local factor is the specific node

30:25.880 --> 30:27.800
where you finally end up making the hole.

30:28.440 --> 30:31.040
A stack of steps is the path you took to get there.

30:31.640 --> 30:33.040
But as you rightly deduced,

30:33.280 --> 30:35.380
every single step recorded in that stack

30:35.380 --> 30:38.140
contains the physical sibling data you bypassed.

30:38.420 --> 30:40.420
It's an ordered list of layer contexts

30:40.420 --> 30:41.980
radiating outward from the hole.

30:42.360 --> 30:44.860
A local factor and a stack of steps.

30:45.820 --> 30:48.400
It's like you're exploring a cave system.

30:48.400 --> 30:50.820
You have your current physical location,

30:51.360 --> 30:52.100
the local factor,

30:52.600 --> 30:55.360
and you have a massive backpack, the stack.

30:56.120 --> 30:58.080
Every time you pass a diverging tunnel,

30:58.560 --> 31:00.800
you take the entire alternate tunnel system,

31:01.340 --> 31:01.940
shrink it down,

31:02.260 --> 31:03.420
and shove it into your backpack.

31:03.780 --> 31:04.740
That's exactly it.

31:05.060 --> 31:06.840
When you want to go back to the surface,

31:07.500 --> 31:09.720
you pull the alternate tunnels out of your backpack

31:09.720 --> 31:11.980
one by one in strict reverse order

31:11.980 --> 31:14.400
and stitch the cave system back together

31:14.400 --> 31:15.600
behind you on your way up.

31:15.600 --> 31:18.180
And the mathematics perfectly describes

31:18.180 --> 31:20.400
that memory management of a branching structure.

31:21.720 --> 31:23.220
But I have to play skeptic for a minute here.

31:23.800 --> 31:26.760
We have derived these flawless algebraic formulas

31:26.760 --> 31:30.820
that dictate how to navigate and rebuild complex data.

31:30.940 --> 31:33.400
But is this actually used?

31:33.780 --> 31:34.600
You mean in practice?

31:34.940 --> 31:35.120
Yeah.

31:35.360 --> 31:37.240
Like, are programmers sitting at their desks

31:37.240 --> 31:39.200
doing calculus on whiteboards

31:39.200 --> 31:40.660
before they write a database query?

31:40.660 --> 31:43.660
Is there a real machine that utilizes this?

31:43.740 --> 31:46.380
Or is this just a purely academic thought experiment?

31:46.940 --> 31:48.640
Oh, it is intensely practical.

31:48.940 --> 31:52.160
And it is the foundation of specific architectures

31:52.160 --> 31:52.980
running right now.

31:53.180 --> 31:54.800
This brings us to the bridge

31:54.800 --> 31:57.180
between theoretical math and functional programming.

31:58.420 --> 32:00.360
Flyxion's paper specifically highlights

32:00.360 --> 32:01.980
concrete implementations,

32:02.160 --> 32:03.840
the most prominent being a data structure

32:03.840 --> 32:04.900
known as the zipper,

32:05.320 --> 32:08.280
which was introduced by Girard Huet in 1997.

32:09.100 --> 32:09.720
The zipper.

32:09.720 --> 32:12.300
The zipper is the practical,

32:13.060 --> 32:15.720
executable embodiment of the calculus we just derived.

32:16.160 --> 32:17.840
It's a great evocative name.

32:18.240 --> 32:19.880
It sounds like exactly what it does,

32:19.980 --> 32:21.720
unzipping a structure to get to the middle

32:21.720 --> 32:23.740
and zipping it back up to close it.

32:24.320 --> 32:26.460
How does a zipper physically operate

32:26.460 --> 32:27.380
in a computer's memory?

32:28.160 --> 32:30.220
A zipper is the programmatic realization

32:30.220 --> 32:31.100
of our derivative.

32:31.500 --> 32:34.100
It is literally defined as a context

32:34.100 --> 32:35.800
paired with a specific value.

32:35.800 --> 32:39.280
It is the hole plus the piece of data

32:39.280 --> 32:40.860
that currently occupies the hole.

32:41.700 --> 32:43.920
The mathematical formulas we just unpacked

32:43.920 --> 32:45.760
map directly to the core functions

32:45.760 --> 32:47.880
a programmer writes to operate a zipper.

32:48.440 --> 32:51.280
The most fundamental of these is the plug function.

32:51.280 --> 32:52.340
The plug function.

32:52.600 --> 32:52.780
Right.

32:53.160 --> 32:55.200
The plug function takes a new replacement value,

32:55.360 --> 32:56.160
your new X,

32:56.840 --> 32:58.960
and it takes the stored context,

32:59.240 --> 33:00.640
your prefix, suffix,

33:00.720 --> 33:02.180
or your stack of tree branches.

33:02.860 --> 33:05.540
It inserts the new value into the focus point,

33:05.820 --> 33:08.740
and then it systematically evaluates the stack,

33:09.080 --> 33:12.020
folding all the retained sibling context back around it,

33:12.120 --> 33:13.040
layer by layer,

33:13.600 --> 33:16.280
perfectly reconstructing the entire original data structure.

33:16.280 --> 33:17.780
Let's talk about why the zipper

33:17.780 --> 33:20.160
was such a revolutionary concept in 1997,

33:20.800 --> 33:22.880
particularly regarding memory efficiency,

33:23.340 --> 33:24.800
because understanding the why

33:24.800 --> 33:26.640
makes the math even more impressive.

33:27.060 --> 33:27.560
Please do.

33:27.740 --> 33:29.500
Let's say you have a list of a million items

33:29.500 --> 33:31.640
in an immutable programming language.

33:32.060 --> 33:34.320
Immutable means you absolutely cannot change

33:34.320 --> 33:35.640
a piece of data once it's created.

33:36.060 --> 33:38.460
If you want to change element 500,000,

33:38.980 --> 33:40.020
you can't just overwrite it.

33:40.380 --> 33:43.040
The computer literally has to copy the entire list,

33:43.140 --> 33:44.160
all one million items,

33:44.160 --> 33:46.140
into a brand new location in memory

33:46.140 --> 33:48.820
just to swap out that one single element.

33:49.200 --> 33:50.660
It's incredibly inefficient.

33:51.020 --> 33:53.380
It takes a massive amount of processing power and time.

33:53.920 --> 33:56.880
It's what computer scientists call an O of N operation.

33:57.540 --> 33:59.320
But if you use a zipper structure,

33:59.700 --> 34:00.980
the paradigm completely flips.

34:01.800 --> 34:02.940
A zipper moves its focus

34:02.940 --> 34:05.280
by physically reversing the pointers in memory.

34:05.480 --> 34:06.220
Right, locally.

34:06.820 --> 34:07.020
Yeah.

34:07.680 --> 34:09.820
If I'm focused on element 500,000,

34:09.820 --> 34:14.020
I have a prefix of 499,999 items

34:14.020 --> 34:15.980
and a suffix of 500,000 items.

34:16.580 --> 34:18.920
If I want to move my focus one step to the right,

34:19.220 --> 34:20.860
to element 500,001,

34:21.100 --> 34:23.280
the computer doesn't copy the whole list.

34:23.940 --> 34:25.960
It simply takes the head off the suffix,

34:26.500 --> 34:27.480
makes it the new focus,

34:27.700 --> 34:29.800
and pushes the old focus onto the end of the prefix.

34:30.560 --> 34:31.660
It's just swapping two pointers.

34:32.200 --> 34:33.920
It turns a massive copying job

34:33.920 --> 34:37.620
into a localized, constant-time, O of 1 operation.

34:37.820 --> 34:38.820
It's lightning fast.

34:38.820 --> 34:40.740
The efficiency gains are massive.

34:41.320 --> 34:43.040
The calculus specifies exactly

34:43.040 --> 34:46.040
what minimal information must be preserved in the context.

34:46.540 --> 34:49.280
And the software engineer implements that context as a zipper

34:49.280 --> 34:51.800
to make local movements incredibly cheap

34:51.800 --> 34:53.200
in terms of memory overhead.

34:53.940 --> 34:55.600
And Flyxion proves the direct linkage

34:55.600 --> 34:57.020
between the math and the code.

34:57.760 --> 34:59.380
The appendix of the paper actually contains

34:59.380 --> 35:01.540
executable code written in Lean 4.

35:01.880 --> 35:03.480
Wait, I need to interrupt here.

35:03.480 --> 35:05.860
For the listener who is familiar with languages

35:05.860 --> 35:08.600
like Python, JavaScript, or C++,

35:09.280 --> 35:11.940
what makes Lean 4 different?

35:12.400 --> 35:13.640
You mentioned earlier to me

35:13.640 --> 35:16.080
that it's a dependently typed programming language

35:16.080 --> 35:17.500
in theorem prover.

35:17.920 --> 35:18.340
That's right.

35:18.580 --> 35:20.460
What does a theorem prover actually do?

35:20.520 --> 35:21.380
Is it writing code,

35:21.440 --> 35:23.200
or is it checking a math test?

35:23.560 --> 35:25.120
It's doing both simultaneously,

35:25.320 --> 35:27.180
which is the sheer beauty of it.

35:27.500 --> 35:29.040
A theorem prover like Lean 4

35:29.040 --> 35:31.240
operates on the Curry-Howard correspondence.

35:32.120 --> 35:34.240
The Curry-Howard correspondence.

35:34.620 --> 35:34.820
Okay.

35:35.280 --> 35:37.500
It's the principle that mathematical proofs

35:37.500 --> 35:38.400
and computer programs

35:38.400 --> 35:40.620
are fundamentally the exact same thing.

35:41.200 --> 35:42.900
Writing a rigorous mathematical proof

35:42.900 --> 35:44.000
that a structure is valid

35:44.000 --> 35:45.720
is identical to writing the code

35:45.720 --> 35:47.060
that compiles that structure.

35:47.500 --> 35:47.760
Wow.

35:48.460 --> 35:50.540
Flyxion wrote a grammar in Lean 4

35:50.540 --> 35:52.740
that actively processes this calculus.

35:53.300 --> 35:54.280
You can feed this program

35:54.280 --> 35:56.720
the pure algebraic definition of a binary tree.

35:56.720 --> 35:58.600
The compiler automatically takes

35:58.600 --> 35:59.740
the mathematical derivative,

36:00.320 --> 36:02.500
calculates the required zipper stack lengths,

36:02.680 --> 36:04.000
saying for this specific tree,

36:04.160 --> 36:06.520
the depths are 2, 2, 1,

36:06.720 --> 36:09.100
and generates the optimal navigation code.

36:09.640 --> 36:11.220
It then runs internal proofs

36:11.220 --> 36:12.780
to guarantee it can replace a leaf

36:12.780 --> 36:14.160
and rebuild the entire tree

36:14.160 --> 36:15.700
without a single memory error.

36:16.320 --> 36:18.060
So the computer is performing

36:18.060 --> 36:20.180
the calculus on the data types

36:20.180 --> 36:23.020
and writing its own mathematically proven

36:23.020 --> 36:24.140
memory management code

36:24.140 --> 36:25.440
based on the result.

36:25.440 --> 36:27.260
It's literally a compiler

36:27.260 --> 36:28.580
that understands calculus.

36:28.720 --> 36:30.240
That is incredible.

36:30.480 --> 36:31.220
It really is.

36:31.760 --> 36:32.580
Before we move into

36:32.580 --> 36:33.840
the final stages of the paper,

36:33.960 --> 36:35.240
there is a brief mention

36:35.240 --> 36:37.100
of an alternative mathematical lens

36:37.100 --> 36:38.260
called containers.

36:38.940 --> 36:40.500
What is the distinction there?

36:40.940 --> 36:42.160
It's not a different truth,

36:42.280 --> 36:44.440
but rather a different geometric approach

36:44.440 --> 36:45.800
to the exact same truth.

36:46.200 --> 36:47.700
The formulation we've been using,

36:47.880 --> 36:49.800
recursive equations and fixed points,

36:50.340 --> 36:52.560
calculates the context layer by layer,

36:52.800 --> 36:53.800
stepping through the data

36:53.800 --> 36:54.780
from the inside out.

36:54.780 --> 36:55.860
The maze method.

36:56.380 --> 36:56.540
Right.

36:57.320 --> 36:58.620
The containers formulation,

36:58.960 --> 37:00.480
developed by researchers Abbott,

37:00.640 --> 37:02.420
Altenkirsch, Ghani, and McBride,

37:02.680 --> 37:03.840
looks at the data type

37:03.840 --> 37:05.100
from a macro perspective.

37:05.700 --> 37:06.620
Instead of layers,

37:06.920 --> 37:08.000
it describes a structure

37:08.000 --> 37:09.920
entirely by its overall shape

37:09.920 --> 37:12.100
and the positions inside that shape.

37:12.240 --> 37:13.180
So pulling back.

37:13.500 --> 37:13.980
Exactly.

37:14.480 --> 37:15.160
For a list,

37:15.380 --> 37:16.840
the shape is just a single integer.

37:17.180 --> 37:17.960
It's total length.

37:17.960 --> 37:20.160
The positions are the index numbers,

37:20.280 --> 37:21.820
zero, one, two, three, and so on.

37:22.340 --> 37:23.940
Taking the derivative of a container

37:23.940 --> 37:26.480
means holding the overall shape constant,

37:26.900 --> 37:28.500
picking one specific position

37:28.500 --> 37:29.820
to act as the whole,

37:30.180 --> 37:31.860
and structurally assigning values

37:31.860 --> 37:33.200
to all the remaining positions.

37:33.400 --> 37:34.840
It's like analyzing a sculpture.

37:35.520 --> 37:37.480
The recursive method is like

37:37.480 --> 37:38.520
walking around the sculpture

37:38.520 --> 37:40.540
inch by inch with a magnifying glass,

37:40.920 --> 37:42.200
recording every bump and curve.

37:42.860 --> 37:44.400
The container method

37:44.400 --> 37:45.580
is like stepping back,

37:45.960 --> 37:46.740
looking at the shadow

37:46.740 --> 37:47.540
it casts on the wall

37:47.540 --> 37:49.240
and deducing its total volume.

37:50.340 --> 37:51.260
They are just different

37:51.260 --> 37:52.480
observational techniques

37:52.480 --> 37:53.440
proving the existence

37:53.440 --> 37:55.180
of the exact same solid object.

37:56.200 --> 37:57.160
The math arrives

37:57.160 --> 37:58.500
at the exact same context

37:58.500 --> 38:00.300
regardless of the method used.

38:00.920 --> 38:01.980
The robustness of the math

38:01.980 --> 38:03.200
is undeniable.

38:04.000 --> 38:05.260
However, up to this point,

38:05.600 --> 38:06.860
we have only conquered

38:06.860 --> 38:08.780
pure structural shapes,

38:09.080 --> 38:09.760
lists and trees.

38:10.080 --> 38:10.760
Just the geometry.

38:10.920 --> 38:11.060
Right.

38:11.060 --> 38:13.500
But data in the real world

38:13.500 --> 38:14.460
of software engineering

38:14.460 --> 38:15.980
isn't always just raw,

38:16.220 --> 38:16.980
physical shape.

38:17.700 --> 38:19.120
Data often has rules.

38:19.500 --> 38:20.760
It has linguistic laws

38:20.760 --> 38:21.680
and semantic meaning

38:21.680 --> 38:22.380
attached to it,

38:22.760 --> 38:23.860
much like the syntax

38:23.860 --> 38:25.460
of a programming language itself.

38:26.180 --> 38:27.500
What happens to our calculus

38:27.500 --> 38:28.600
when the data

38:28.600 --> 38:29.480
isn't just structural,

38:29.860 --> 38:31.360
but has logical laws

38:31.360 --> 38:32.980
it absolutely must obey?

38:33.180 --> 38:33.860
Well, that feels like

38:33.860 --> 38:34.880
a massive complication.

38:35.760 --> 38:36.720
Navigating a structure

38:36.720 --> 38:37.420
is one thing,

38:37.820 --> 38:40.080
but actively enforcing rules

38:40.080 --> 38:41.140
while you navigate it?

38:41.400 --> 38:42.600
How does the theory address that?

38:42.640 --> 38:43.600
This is where we engage

38:43.600 --> 38:45.140
with the final major hurdle

38:45.140 --> 38:46.180
of Flyxion's paper,

38:46.460 --> 38:47.460
dealing with syntax,

38:47.920 --> 38:48.180
binding,

38:48.440 --> 38:49.860
and the three layers of truth.

38:50.400 --> 38:51.800
The three layers of truth.

38:52.340 --> 38:52.860
I like that.

38:52.860 --> 38:54.200
When we discuss

38:54.200 --> 38:54.840
the syntax

38:54.840 --> 38:56.420
of a programming language,

38:56.640 --> 38:57.500
we have to deal

38:57.500 --> 38:58.180
with the problem

38:58.180 --> 38:59.560
of variable binding.

39:00.140 --> 39:01.320
This adds a profound layer

39:01.320 --> 39:02.680
of semantic complexity

39:02.680 --> 39:04.860
on top of the structural complexity.

39:04.860 --> 39:07.680
Consider a dependent function type,

39:07.820 --> 39:09.020
typically written as

39:09.020 --> 39:12.580
parentheses x colon a arrow b.

39:13.300 --> 39:14.220
Okay, let's break that down.

39:14.660 --> 39:15.500
In this expression,

39:16.080 --> 39:17.440
a represents the domain.

39:17.880 --> 39:19.160
It defines the strict rules

39:19.160 --> 39:20.720
for what type of thing

39:20.720 --> 39:22.680
the variable x is allowed to be.

39:23.360 --> 39:25.600
B is the body of the function.

39:26.320 --> 39:28.580
Crucially, b acts as a scope.

39:28.960 --> 39:30.400
It is an enclosed environment

39:30.400 --> 39:31.960
where the variable x

39:31.960 --> 39:33.800
is legally allowed to exist,

39:34.280 --> 39:34.940
be referenced,

39:35.180 --> 39:36.040
and be manipulated.

39:36.820 --> 39:38.380
However, the domain a

39:38.380 --> 39:39.940
is evaluated before

39:39.940 --> 39:41.020
the variable x

39:41.020 --> 39:42.240
is even instantiated.

39:42.660 --> 39:43.840
Therefore, inside a,

39:43.840 --> 39:45.220
the variable x

39:45.220 --> 39:47.000
technically does not exist yet.

39:47.640 --> 39:48.280
Okay, this is getting

39:48.280 --> 39:49.320
highly abstract,

39:49.640 --> 39:50.360
so I want to introduce

39:50.360 --> 39:51.460
an analogy to ground

39:51.460 --> 39:53.560
this concept of scope and binding.

39:54.440 --> 39:55.140
Let's call it

39:55.140 --> 39:56.460
the legal contract analogy.

39:56.980 --> 39:57.580
Let's hear it.

39:57.800 --> 39:59.080
Imagine you were a lawyer

39:59.080 --> 40:00.100
editing a massive,

40:00.100 --> 40:01.800
highly complex legal contract.

40:02.440 --> 40:04.000
The overall physical structure

40:04.000 --> 40:04.640
of the contract,

40:04.800 --> 40:05.340
the paragraphs,

40:05.680 --> 40:06.600
the nested bullet points,

40:06.700 --> 40:07.640
the grammar of the sentences,

40:08.160 --> 40:09.800
that is our pure data structure.

40:10.180 --> 40:11.440
It's the tree we just learned

40:11.440 --> 40:11.960
how to navigate

40:11.960 --> 40:13.280
with our calculus zipper.

40:13.720 --> 40:14.480
Structure, right.

40:14.720 --> 40:17.100
Now, imagine a specific clause

40:17.100 --> 40:17.720
in the contract

40:17.720 --> 40:19.200
operates like our function,

40:19.620 --> 40:21.380
x colon a arrow b.

40:22.320 --> 40:24.120
A is the definition section

40:24.120 --> 40:25.420
on page one of the contract.

40:25.920 --> 40:27.320
It formally defines a term.

40:27.420 --> 40:28.140
Let's say it defines

40:28.140 --> 40:29.200
the corporation,

40:29.200 --> 40:30.260
and it's referring

40:30.260 --> 40:31.760
exclusively to a company

40:31.760 --> 40:32.880
registered in Delaware.

40:33.580 --> 40:33.700
Okay.

40:34.500 --> 40:36.280
B is the rest of the contract,

40:36.660 --> 40:37.700
pages two through ten.

40:38.340 --> 40:39.820
These pages constantly reference

40:39.820 --> 40:40.820
and use the term

40:40.820 --> 40:41.800
the corporation.

40:42.240 --> 40:43.620
Those pages are the scope.

40:44.180 --> 40:44.960
Here's the problem.

40:45.500 --> 40:47.620
If I use my structural zipper

40:47.620 --> 40:48.920
to navigate backward

40:48.920 --> 40:49.680
to page one,

40:49.900 --> 40:51.000
the domain A,

40:51.500 --> 40:52.760
and I completely rewrite

40:52.760 --> 40:53.620
the definition of

40:53.620 --> 40:54.500
the corporation

40:54.500 --> 40:56.160
to refer to a company

40:56.160 --> 40:57.200
in California.

40:57.840 --> 40:59.080
The physical structure

40:59.080 --> 40:59.540
of the contract

40:59.540 --> 41:00.860
is still perfectly fine.

41:01.320 --> 41:01.960
The paragraphs

41:01.960 --> 41:02.760
are still paragraphs.

41:03.260 --> 41:04.700
The tree is completely intact.

41:05.220 --> 41:06.380
The geometry holds.

41:06.620 --> 41:07.920
But by changing

41:07.920 --> 41:08.480
that definition

41:08.480 --> 41:09.320
on page one,

41:09.900 --> 41:10.700
I might have just made

41:10.700 --> 41:11.900
the clauses on page ten,

41:12.040 --> 41:12.520
the body,

41:12.800 --> 41:13.020
be,

41:13.540 --> 41:14.280
legally invalid

41:14.280 --> 41:15.560
because page ten

41:15.560 --> 41:16.200
was relying on

41:16.200 --> 41:17.500
the old Delaware definition

41:17.500 --> 41:18.500
that I just destroyed.

41:19.160 --> 41:20.580
The structural derivative

41:20.580 --> 41:21.280
is intact,

41:21.280 --> 41:22.360
but the meaning

41:22.360 --> 41:22.980
and the rules

41:22.980 --> 41:23.580
are broken.

41:24.140 --> 41:25.060
The contract analogy

41:25.060 --> 41:26.620
perfectly encapsulates

41:26.620 --> 41:27.840
Flyxion's central thesis

41:27.840 --> 41:28.600
on syntax.

41:29.020 --> 41:29.240
In fact,

41:29.360 --> 41:30.020
Flyxion states

41:30.020 --> 41:30.720
this distinction

41:30.720 --> 41:32.380
is the organizing claim

41:32.380 --> 41:33.880
of the entire essay.

41:34.300 --> 41:34.980
When we deal with

41:34.980 --> 41:35.840
syntax and meaning,

41:36.000 --> 41:37.100
we simply cannot rely

41:37.100 --> 41:37.960
solely on the

41:37.960 --> 41:38.780
structural derivative.

41:39.260 --> 41:40.340
We must deliberately

41:40.340 --> 41:41.700
separate our understanding

41:41.700 --> 41:43.740
into three distinct

41:43.740 --> 41:45.080
interacting layers

41:45.080 --> 41:45.900
of information.

41:47.020 --> 41:48.480
The three layers.

41:49.120 --> 41:49.820
Let's go through them.

41:49.820 --> 41:51.060
Layer one

41:51.060 --> 41:52.220
is reconstruction.

41:53.100 --> 41:54.020
This is the mathematical

41:54.020 --> 41:55.160
derivative we've spent

41:55.160 --> 41:56.580
our time deriving today.

41:56.900 --> 41:57.760
This is pure

41:57.760 --> 41:58.640
physical structure.

41:58.860 --> 41:59.720
It answers the question,

42:00.260 --> 42:01.460
what physical data blocks

42:01.460 --> 42:02.320
do I need to rebuild

42:02.320 --> 42:02.740
the tree?

42:02.940 --> 42:03.680
It is the paragraphs

42:03.680 --> 42:04.480
and bullet points

42:04.480 --> 42:05.140
of your contract.

42:05.620 --> 42:05.760
Okay.

42:06.080 --> 42:07.040
Layer one is structure.

42:07.640 --> 42:08.260
What's layer two?

42:08.820 --> 42:10.100
Layer two is scope.

42:10.660 --> 42:11.320
This is managed

42:11.320 --> 42:12.340
by what the paper calls

42:12.340 --> 42:13.740
a binder-aware zipper.

42:14.080 --> 42:15.860
This is the linguistic context.

42:16.100 --> 42:16.580
It answers,

42:16.900 --> 42:17.600
what variables

42:17.600 --> 42:18.820
am I legally allowed

42:18.820 --> 42:19.800
to use or reference

42:19.800 --> 42:21.460
at this exact coordinate

42:21.460 --> 42:22.240
in the structure?

42:22.660 --> 42:23.460
This requires

42:23.460 --> 42:24.880
an ambient context,

42:25.180 --> 42:26.440
an active running list

42:26.440 --> 42:27.360
of names and memory.

42:27.820 --> 42:28.620
In your analogy,

42:28.760 --> 42:29.880
this is the legal system

42:29.880 --> 42:30.700
actively tracking

42:30.700 --> 42:31.480
whether the term

42:31.480 --> 42:32.380
the corporation

42:32.380 --> 42:33.780
is currently defined

42:33.780 --> 42:34.860
in the specific section

42:34.860 --> 42:35.520
you are editing.

42:35.800 --> 42:35.900
Right.

42:36.540 --> 42:37.440
And layer three?

42:37.860 --> 42:39.280
Layer three is fit.

42:39.580 --> 42:40.120
This is handled

42:40.120 --> 42:41.400
by the typing judgment

42:41.400 --> 42:42.240
of the compiler.

42:42.640 --> 42:43.160
This answers

42:43.160 --> 42:44.020
the final question.

42:44.460 --> 42:45.440
Does the new data

42:45.440 --> 42:46.460
I put into the hole

42:46.460 --> 42:48.060
actually make logical sense?

42:48.060 --> 42:49.080
And crucially,

42:49.300 --> 42:50.140
does it violate

42:50.140 --> 42:51.260
or invalidate

42:51.260 --> 42:52.460
any of the retained parts

42:52.460 --> 42:53.660
I saved in my context?

42:54.040 --> 42:55.540
It is the final legal review,

42:55.660 --> 42:56.800
checking if page 10

42:56.800 --> 42:57.460
still complies

42:57.460 --> 42:57.920
with the edits

42:57.920 --> 42:58.780
made to page one.

42:59.340 --> 42:59.960
Reconstruction,

43:00.460 --> 43:00.780
scope,

43:01.320 --> 43:01.840
and fit.

43:02.540 --> 43:03.140
Structure,

43:03.520 --> 43:03.800
language,

43:04.260 --> 43:04.800
and logic.

43:05.220 --> 43:06.440
It brings a lot of clarity

43:06.440 --> 43:07.480
when you break it down

43:07.480 --> 43:09.900
into those three distinct mandates.

43:09.900 --> 43:11.940
I noticed in the paper

43:11.940 --> 43:13.360
Flyxion deliberately

43:13.360 --> 43:14.800
sets aside deeper complexities

43:14.800 --> 43:17.040
like locally nameless disciplines

43:17.040 --> 43:17.720
early on

43:17.720 --> 43:19.240
in order to isolate

43:19.240 --> 43:20.000
that first layer,

43:20.100 --> 43:21.380
the pure structural derivative.

43:22.060 --> 43:22.740
I'm really glad

43:22.740 --> 43:23.260
you brought up

43:23.260 --> 43:24.520
locally nameless disciplines.

43:24.800 --> 43:25.880
It's a dense term,

43:26.060 --> 43:26.680
but it highlights

43:26.680 --> 43:27.780
exactly why separating

43:27.780 --> 43:28.940
these layers is so vital.

43:29.660 --> 43:31.360
I have to admit,

43:31.720 --> 43:33.160
when I read locally nameless,

43:33.260 --> 43:34.300
my eyes crossed a bit.

43:34.800 --> 43:35.560
Can you unpack

43:35.560 --> 43:37.000
what that actually means

43:37.000 --> 43:38.140
for a compiler?

43:38.800 --> 43:39.300
Certainly.

43:39.580 --> 43:40.180
In programming,

43:40.440 --> 43:41.420
variable shadowing

43:41.420 --> 43:42.900
is a massive headache.

43:43.240 --> 43:44.100
Imagine you have

43:44.100 --> 43:45.140
a nested function.

43:45.800 --> 43:46.560
The outer function

43:46.560 --> 43:47.360
defines a variable

43:47.360 --> 43:48.260
named x.

43:48.620 --> 43:49.720
The inner function

43:49.720 --> 43:50.960
also defines

43:50.960 --> 43:52.280
a completely different variable,

43:52.620 --> 43:54.540
but it's also named x.

43:54.920 --> 43:55.560
Oh, that sounds

43:55.560 --> 43:56.280
like a disaster.

43:56.600 --> 43:57.100
It is.

43:57.500 --> 43:58.400
How does the computer

43:58.400 --> 44:00.440
know which x you mean

44:00.440 --> 44:01.140
when you are deep

44:01.140 --> 44:02.220
inside the inner function?

